No.

**Counterexample.** Let $\Omega = (0,1) \subset \mathbb{R}$, a bounded domain. Choose a smooth bump $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R})$ with $\operatorname{supp}\varphi \subset [0,1]$ and $\int_{\mathbb{R}} \varphi = 1$ (e.g. a standard mollifier scaled to have support in $[0,1]$). Define

$$f_k(x) = k\,\varphi(kx), \qquad x \in (0,1).$$

Each $f_k$ is smooth on $(0,1)$, and since $\operatorname{supp}\varphi \subset [0,1]$, we have $\operatorname{supp} f_k \subset [0, 1/k] \subset (0,1)$ for $k \ge 2$. By the change of variables $u = kx$,

$$\int_0^1 f_k(x)\,dx = \int_0^1 k\,\varphi(kx)\,dx = \int_0^k \varphi(u)\,du = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \varphi(u)\,du = 1,$$

so $\int_\Omega f_k = 1$ for all $k$.

**Uniform convergence on compact subsets.** Let $K \subset (0,1)$ be compact. Then $K \subset [\varepsilon, 1-\varepsilon]$ for some $\varepsilon > 0$. For $k > 1/\varepsilon$, the support $[0,1/k]$ of $f_k$ is disjoint from $[\varepsilon, 1-\varepsilon]$, hence $f_k \equiv 0$ on $K$. Therefore $f_k \to 0$ uniformly on $K$ (in fact $f_k = 0$ on $K$ for all large $k$).

Thus $f_k \to f \equiv 0$ uniformly on every compact subset of $\Omega$, with $f \in L^1(\Omega)$ smooth, yet

$$\int_\Omega f = \int_0^1 0\,dx = 0 \neq 1.$$

The implication fails because locally uniform convergence on a bounded domain does not control mass that "escapes" toward the boundary. Hence the answer is **no**.

